-- Q16 [Endoderm Derivatives | easy | Skill 1 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub16$Endoderm Derivatives$sub16$, $qt16$During the third week of human development, a researcher tracks the cells that will go on to form the simple epithelium lining the lumen of the small intestine. From which embryonic source do these gut-lining cells arise?$qt16$, $op16$[{"label": "A", "text": "The ectoderm, the outermost germ layer, which forms the epidermis and the central nervous system"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The mesoderm, the middle germ layer, which forms the skeleton, muscles, and connective tissue"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The neural crest, a migratory population that forms peripheral neurons and pigment cells"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The endoderm, the innermost germ layer, which forms the epithelial lining of the gastrointestinal tract"}]$op16$::jsonb, 'D', $ex16$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is D because the endoderm is the innermost of the three primary germ layers and is the lineage that produces the epithelial lining of the gut tube. After gastrulation, each germ layer is programmed to give rise to specific structures: the endoderm "goes on to form the epithelial lining of the gastrointestinal tract, liver, and pancreas, as well as the lungs," whereas the ectoderm and mesoderm produce body-surface, nervous, and structural tissues. The cells lining the small-intestinal lumen are therefore endodermal in origin, even though the muscular wall surrounding that lining is mesodermal. (Choice A) The ectoderm is the outermost layer and "gives rise to cell lineages that differentiate to become the central and peripheral nervous systems, sensory organs, epidermis, hair, and nails," not the gut lining. (Choice B) The mesoderm builds the muscular wall and other support tissues, becoming "the skeleton, muscles, connective tissue, heart, blood vessels, and kidneys," but not the inner epithelial lining. (Choice C) The neural crest is an ectoderm-derived migratory population that develops "into several parts of the peripheral nervous system (PNS)" and melanocytes, not the digestive-tract epithelium. (Choice D) The endoderm is correct because it is the innermost germ layer that forms the epithelial lining of the gastrointestinal tract. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established developmental origin of the gut epithelium from the endodermal germ layer.$ex16$, 'easy', '2C', $cc16$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc16$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a16A$assigns the gut lining to the outermost (ectoderm) layer$a16A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a16B$names the layer that builds the muscular wall rather than the lining$a16B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a16C$names a real ectoderm-derived lineage that is not the gut-forming germ layer$a16C$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Endoderm Derivatives | easy | Skill 1 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub17$Endoderm Derivatives$sub17$, $qt17$During the fourth week of development, a dome-shaped lung bud bulges from the foregut and elongates to form the laryngotracheal bud, which gives rise to the trachea, bronchi, and the epithelial lining of the lungs. From which embryonic germ layer is this epithelial lining of the lower respiratory tract derived?$qt17$, $op17$[{"label": "A", "text": "Ectoderm, which forms the epidermis, hair, nails, and the nervous system"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mesoderm, which forms the heart, blood vessels, and skeletal and smooth muscle"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Endoderm, which forms the epithelial lining of the gastrointestinal tract and the lungs"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Trophoblast, which forms the chorionic sac and the fetal portion of the placenta"}]$op17$::jsonb, 'C', $ex17$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is C because the lung bud arises from the foregut, which is endoderm, and the endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the lungs. The text states that "the lung bud is a dome-shaped structure composed of tissue that bulges from the foregut. The foregut is endoderm just inferior to the pharyngeal pouches" and confirms that "the endoderm goes on to form the epithelial lining of the gastrointestinal tract, liver, and pancreas, as well as the lungs." Thus the trachea, bronchi, and lung epithelium that develop from the laryngotracheal bud all trace back to foregut endoderm. (Choice A) Ectoderm gives rise to the central and peripheral nervous systems, sensory organs, epidermis, hair, and nails, the outer covering of the body, not the airway lining. (Choice B) Mesoderm becomes the skeleton, muscles, connective tissue, heart, and blood vessels, which surround the airways but do not form the airway epithelium itself. (Choice C) Endoderm is correct because the foregut-derived lung bud forms the epithelial lining of the lower respiratory tract. (Choice D) The trophoblast is an early extraembryonic lineage that develops into the chorionic sac and the fetal portion of the placenta, not a germ layer that lines the lungs. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established developmental origin of the respiratory epithelium from the foregut endoderm.$ex17$, 'easy', '2C', $cc17$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc17$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
