-- Q18 [Endoderm Derivatives | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub18$Endoderm Derivatives$sub18$, $qt18$A developmental biologist tracks the embryonic gut tube and observes the pancreas and the thyroid gland forming as outgrowths that bud directly off the wall of this primitive digestive tube. From which primary germ layer are these two budding endocrine glands derived?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "Endoderm, because the gut tube it forms also buds off the thyroid, parathyroid, pancreas, and thymus"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mesoderm, because it forms the steroid-secreting gonads and the adrenal cortex"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ectoderm, because it forms the pituitary gland and the adrenal medulla"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neural crest, because it forms the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla"}]$op18$::jsonb, 'A', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is A because the pancreas and thyroid bud from the endodermal gut tube, the same layer that the textbook states "gives rise to the thyroid and parathyroid glands, as well as the pancreas and the thymus." The endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive tract, and endocrine glands such as the thyroid and pancreas arise as outgrowths of that gut-tube epithelium; recognizing that a gland buds off the gut tube is therefore the inference that pins its origin to the endoderm. The other germ layers contribute different endocrine structures, so each alternative names a real gland but assigns the wrong source layer for the pancreas and thyroid. (Choice A) is correct: the endoderm forms the gut-tube epithelium that buds off the thyroid, parathyroid, pancreas, and thymus. (Choice B) is wrong because the mesoderm forms the steroid-hormone glands—the gonads and adrenal cortex—not the gut-derived pancreas or thyroid. (Choice C) is wrong because the ectoderm gives rise to the pituitary gland and the adrenal medulla, which are not gut-tube derivatives. (Choice D) is wrong because the neural crest forms the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, an endocrine lineage unrelated to the origin of the pancreas or thyroid. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires using the observation that a gland buds off the embryonic gut tube to infer the underlying germ-layer origin, rather than simply recalling a stated fact.$ex18$, 'medium', '2C', $cc18$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a18B$Correctly identifies a real endoderm-vs-mesoderm split in endocrine origin but applies the mesoderm fact to the wrong (steroid) glands$a18B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a18C$Names true ectoderm-derived endocrine structures (pituitary, adrenal medulla) that are not the pancreas or thyroid$a18C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a18D$Names a genuine endocrine-precursor lineage (neural crest to chromaffin cells) unrelated to the gut-tube origin asked about$a18D$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Endoderm Derivatives | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub19$Endoderm Derivatives$sub19$, $qt19$A histologist examines a small blood vessel and identifies its inner lining as a continuous sheet of flat, simple squamous epithelial cells called the endothelium. Asked to name the embryonic germ layer from which this vessel-lining endothelium arises, the histologist should answer that it is:$qt19$, $op19$[{"label": "A", "text": "endodermal, because the endoderm forms the internal epithelial linings of the body's tubes and cavities"}, {"label": "B", "text": "mesodermal, because the endothelium that lines blood and lymphatic vessels derives from the mesoderm"}, {"label": "C", "text": "ectodermal, because the ectoderm forms the body's outer covering and surface epithelia"}, {"label": "D", "text": "endodermal, because the endoderm gives rise to the gastrointestinal and respiratory epithelial linings"}]$op19$::jsonb, 'B', $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is B because the endothelium that lines the blood and lymphatic vessels (and the heart chambers) develops from the mesoderm, not from the endoderm, despite the similar "endo-" prefix and its epithelial appearance. The text states that "the epithelium that lines vessels in the lymphatic and cardiovascular system derives from the mesoderm and is called an endothelium," and that the mesoderm "gives rise to ... blood cells" and "most other visceral organs." The prefix of "endothelium" is a naming convention for the lining of internal vascular and serous spaces; it does not signal an endodermal origin, so the histologist must discriminate developmental layer from word root. (Choice A) Endoderm does form many internal epithelial linings, but vascular endothelium is the specific exception that comes from mesoderm, so naming endoderm here is the classic prefix trap. (Choice B) is correct because the cardiovascular and lymphatic vessel lining is explicitly assigned to the mesoderm by the text. (Choice C) Ectoderm does generate the outer epithelial covering of the body surface, but blood-vessel endothelium is not a surface epithelium and is not ectodermal. (Choice D) The endoderm does give rise to the digestive- and respiratory-tract linings, but that is the wrong germ layer for a blood vessel, which is lined by mesoderm-derived endothelium. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires the student to apply germ-layer assignment rules to a specific structure and resolve a misleading prefix in order to infer the correct developmental origin.$ex19$, 'medium', '2C', $cc19$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a19A$prefix trap: assuming "endothelium" implies an endodermal origin$a19A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a19C$true ectoderm fact (surface epithelium) applied to the wrong structure$a19C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a19D$correct endoderm derivative (GI/respiratory lining) named for the wrong layer$a19D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Endoderm Derivatives | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub20$Endoderm Derivatives$sub20$, $qt20$A geneticist studies an animal embryo carrying a mutation that silences a transcription factor expressed exclusively in the endoderm, blocking that germ layer from differentiating normally. The researcher then examines the wall of the developing small intestine, which contains both an inner epithelial lining and an outer smooth-muscle muscularis. What does the geneticist most likely observe in this intestinal wall?$qt20$, $op20$[{"label": "A", "text": "Both the inner epithelial lining and the smooth-muscle muscularis fail to form, because the entire wall of the intestine is built from endoderm."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The smooth-muscle muscularis fails to form while the inner epithelial lining develops normally, because the intestinal muscle is the endodermal contribution to the wall."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Only the endothelium of the blood vessels running through the wall fails to form, because endoderm builds the lining of every tube within the intestinal wall."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The inner epithelial lining fails to form while the smooth-muscle muscularis develops normally, because the lining is endoderm-derived but the muscle arises from mesoderm."}]$op20$::jsonb, 'D', $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is D because the intestinal wall is a mixed-origin organ: its inner epithelial (mucosal) lining is endoderm-derived, whereas its smooth-muscle muscularis arises from mesoderm, so silencing an endoderm-only factor disrupts the lining but spares the muscle. OpenStax Biology 2e states that "the endoderm gives rise to the lining of the digestive tract (including the stomach, intestines, liver, and pancreas)" but that the mesoderm "gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues (including the cardiac tissues and muscles of the intestines)," explicitly assigning the two tissue layers to two different germ layers. OpenStax A&P 2e reinforces this, noting the endoderm "is responsible for generating the lining tissues...such as the mucosae of the digestive and respiratory systems," while "the mesoderm...gives rise to most of the muscle and connective tissues." Because the mutation is restricted to the endoderm, only the endoderm-derived mucosal epithelium is affected. (Choice A) is wrong because the whole gut wall is not endoderm; the muscularis is mesodermal, so it would not fail. (Choice B) is wrong because it reverses the assignment—muscle is mesodermal, not the endodermal contribution, so the muscularis would survive and the lining would fail, not the opposite. (Choice C) is wrong because vascular endothelium derives from mesoderm, not endoderm, so the vessels in the wall would be unaffected by an endoderm-only defect. (Choice D) is correct because the endoderm-derived mucosal lining fails while the mesoderm-derived muscularis develops normally. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying germ-layer origin rules to a novel mixed-origin organ and predicting which tissue layer a layer-specific mutation will disrupt.$ex20$, 'hard', '2C', $cc20$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
