UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a20B$Swaps which layer endoderm builds, assigning the intestinal muscle to endoderm and the lining to mesoderm.$a20B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a20C$Correctly notes endoderm forms internal linings but wrongly assigns vascular endothelium (mesodermal) to endoderm.$a20C$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Neurulation & Neural Crest | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub21$Neurulation & Neural Crest$sub21$, $qt21$During vertebrate organogenesis, an embryologist wants to identify the embryonic structure whose signal triggers the overlying ectoderm to roll up into the neural tube. Which structure provides this inductive signal?$qt21$, $op21$[{"label": "A", "text": "The notochord, a mesoderm-derived rod, induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural tube."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The somites, blocks of mesoderm flanking the neural tube, induce the overlying ectoderm to form the neural tube."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The primitive gut, an endoderm-derived tube, induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural tube."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The neural crest, a cluster of cells at the fold tips, induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural tube."}]$op21$::jsonb, 'A', $ex21$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is A because the notochord is the mesoderm-derived rod that induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural tube. OpenStax Biology 2e states that the notochord "is a flexible, rod-shaped mesodermal structure" and that in vertebrates it "is present only during embryonic development, at which time it induces the development of the neural tube." OpenStax A&P 2e confirms the spatial relationship: "a rod-like structure called the notochord develops dorsally... The tissue overlying the notochord enlarges and forms the neural tube." Thus the notochord is the upstream inducer, while the neural tube, neural folds, and neural crest are downstream products of this signaling. (Choice A) Correct: the notochord is the mesodermal rod that induces neural tube formation in the overlying ectoderm. (Choice B) The somites are real mesodermal blocks, but they differentiate into vertebrae, ribs, dermis, and skeletal muscle rather than inducing neurulation. (Choice C) The primitive gut is the endodermal tube of the digestive tract and is not the inducer of the neural tube. (Choice D) The neural crest is a product of neurulation that pinches off from the neural folds after the tube forms, so it cannot be the upstream inducer. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the specific textbook fact that the notochord induces neural tube formation.$ex21$, 'easy', '2C', $cc21$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc21$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a21B$Real flanking mesodermal structure (somites) substituted for the inducer, but somites give rise to vertebrae/muscle/dermis and do not drive neurulation$a21B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a21C$Wrong germ layer assigned as inducer — the endodermal gut tube confused with the mesodermal notochord$a21C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a21D$Downstream product of neurulation (neural crest) mistaken for the upstream inducer it actually arises from$a21D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [Neurulation & Neural Crest | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub22$Neurulation & Neural Crest$sub22$, $qt22$A developmental biologist uses a fine laser to selectively destroy the cells at the tips of the neural folds in a vertebrate embryo in the moment just before those cells detach from the ectoderm and migrate away. The neural folds still fuse normally into a tube beneath the surface ectoderm. She then predicts which structures will fail to form in the resulting embryo.$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "The brain and spinal cord fail to form, while the peripheral ganglia still develop normally."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The peripheral sensory and autonomic ganglia fail to form, while the brain and spinal cord still develop normally."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The notochord fails to form, while both the central and peripheral nervous systems develop normally."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The surface epidermis fails to form, while the entire nervous system develops normally."}]$op22$::jsonb, 'B', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is B because the cells destroyed are the neural crest, whose derivatives are peripheral structures, whereas the central nervous system is built by the neural tube itself, which the scenario says still forms normally. As the neural folds converge, the underlying tissue forms the neural tube, and the cells from the neural fold tips then separate from the ectoderm to form the neural crest, which migrates away from the embryonic CNS and develops into parts of the peripheral nervous system, including sensory and autonomic ganglia (A&P 2e lines 23166-23170). Because the ablation removes only the crest just before delamination and leaves the tube intact, the brain (anterior neural tube) and spinal cord (posterior neural tube) still develop, while the PNS derivatives are lost (A&P 2e line 23184). (Choice A) is reversed: the brain and spinal cord come from the neural tube, which forms normally here, so the CNS is unaffected and the crest-derived peripheral ganglia are what is lost. (Choice B) is correct because the neural crest gives rise to peripheral ganglia, and its loss removes the PNS while sparing the tube-derived CNS. (Choice C) is wrong because the notochord is a mesodermal structure that forms before and induces the neural tube, so destroying neural-fold ectoderm cannot prevent notochord formation. (Choice D) is wrong because the epidermis arises from the non-neural ectoderm at the edges of the neural plate, not from the migrating crest cells, so the skin still forms. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the downstream developmental consequence of a targeted cell ablation by mapping the ablated cell population to its specific germ-layer-derived lineage rather than recalling a single fact.$ex22$, 'medium', '2C', $cc22$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a22A$Swaps which neural structure the ablated cells give rise to: assigns the CNS (tube product) to the crest and the PNS to the tube.$a22A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a22C$Treats the mesoderm-derived notochord as a derivative of, or dependent on, the ablated ectodermal neural-fold cells, ignoring that it forms earlier and from a different germ layer.$a22C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a22D$Names the other ectodermal derivative (epidermis) but wrongly sources it from neural crest rather than from the non-neural ectoderm at the plate edges.$a22D$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Neurulation & Neural Crest | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub23$Neurulation & Neural Crest$sub23$, $qt23$During neurulation in a vertebrate embryo, a cluster of cells pinches off from the dorsal neural folds, migrates to the developing skin, and matures into a pigment-producing melanocyte residing in the basal layer of the epidermis. From which primary germ layer did this melanocyte ultimately originate?$qt23$, $op23$[{"label": "A", "text": "Mesoderm, because pigment-producing connective-tissue cells of the skin arise from the middle germ layer"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Endoderm, because the cell lines a surface exposed to the environment like other endodermal epithelia"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Ectoderm, because the melanocyte arose from neural crest cells that delaminate from the ectoderm-derived neural folds"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Mesoderm, because melanocytes share a lineage with the somite-derived dermis they migrate into and populate"}]$op23$::jsonb, 'C', $ex23$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is C because the melanocyte traces back through the neural crest to the ectoderm, the outermost of the three germ layers. During neurulation a portion of the ectoderm forms the neural plate, which folds into the neural folds; cells "from the neural folds then separate from the ectoderm to form a cluster of cells referred to as the neural crest," and "Many tissues that are not part of the nervous system also arise from the neural crest, such as craniofacial cartilage and bone, and melanocytes" (A&P 2e, lines 23166-23171). Because the neural crest delaminates from the ectoderm-derived neural folds, its melanocyte descendants are ectodermal in origin even though they end up living in the skin's basal layer alongside cells of other lineages. The ectoderm is also independently described as developing into "the integumentary system (the skin) and the nervous system" (A&P 2e, lines 23157-23158), consistent with this crest-mediated route. (Choice A) is incorrect because the mesoderm gives rise to muscle and connective tissues, not to pigment cells; placing the melanocyte in mesoderm mistakes its skin residence for a connective-tissue identity. (Choice B) is incorrect because the endoderm gives rise to the lining of internal cavities such as the digestive and respiratory tracts, not to surface pigment cells of the skin. (Choice C) is correct because the melanocyte descends from neural-crest cells that separate from the ectoderm-derived neural folds, making the ectoderm its germ layer of origin. (Choice D) is incorrect because, although the surrounding dermis is largely mesoderm-derived connective tissue, the melanocytes that migrate into and populate the skin originate from the neural crest and thus from ectoderm, not from the somite lineage of the dermis. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires tracing a migrating cell's lineage backward through the steps of neurulation to assign it to the correct germ layer rather than recalling a single fact.$ex23$, 'medium', '2C', $cc23$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc23$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a23A$Treats a cell as belonging to the germ layer of the tissue it resides in (skin connective tissue = mesoderm), ignoring its neural-crest origin$a23A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a23B$Misassigns a surface-associated cell to endoderm, confusing 'surface/lining' with the innermost layer that lines internal cavities$a23B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a23D$Uses a true fact (dermis is mesoderm/somite-derived) but misapplies it to the migrating melanocyte, which is crest/ectoderm-derived$a23D$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Neurulation & Neural Crest | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub24$Neurulation & Neural Crest$sub24$, $qt24$During a study of a teratogen that selectively halts the delamination and migration of neural crest cells, an investigator examines the adrenal glands of affected embryos and finds that the steroid-secreting outer region develops normally. Predicting the fate of the gland's inner region, the investigator should expect that:$qt24$, $op24$[{"label": "A", "text": "the entire adrenal gland, both the outer region and the inner region, is ablated, because both regions are populated by migrating neural crest cells."}, {"label": "B", "text": "the outer steroid-secreting region is ablated while the catecholamine-secreting inner region develops normally, because only the outer region requires crest migration."}, {"label": "C", "text": "the catecholamine-secreting inner region develops normally while only the sympathetic ganglia that innervate it are ablated, because the ganglia alone are crest-derived."}, {"label": "D", "text": "the catecholamine-secreting chromaffin cells of the inner region are ablated, because they arise from the neural crest along with the sympathetic ganglia, whereas the outer region forms from mesoderm."}]$op24$::jsonb, 'D', $ex24$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is D because the adrenal gland is a mixed-lineage organ in which the two regions trace to two different germ layers, and only the crest-derived region depends on the migration the teratogen blocks. The text states that the adrenal medulla's chromaffin cells "are neurosecretory cells that develop from the neural crest along with the sympathetic ganglia," and separately that "the adrenal cortex [arises] from the mesoderm and the adrenal medulla from ectoderm neural cells." Because the neural crest "migrates away from the nascent... central nervous system," a teratogen that blocks crest migration prevents these cells from reaching and forming the medulla, so the catecholamine-secreting chromaffin cells are ablated. The cortex, arising from mesoderm rather than from migrating crest, is unaffected and develops normally, consistent with the observed normal outer region. (Choice A) is wrong because only the medulla is crest-derived; the cortex arises from mesoderm and would not be ablated by blocking crest migration. (Choice B) reverses the dependency: it is the medulla, not the mesodermal cortex, whose formation requires neural crest migration. (Choice C) is incomplete because, although the sympathetic ganglia are indeed crest-derived and would be lost, the medullary chromaffin cells share that same neural crest origin and would also be ablated, so the medulla cannot develop normally. (Choice D) correctly assigns the medullary chromaffin cells to the neural crest and the cortex to mesoderm, predicting selective loss of the medulla. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying germ-layer origin data to a novel teratogen scenario and predicting which mixed-lineage component of an organ is selectively spared versus ablated.$ex24$, 'hard', '2C', $cc24$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc24$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'partial_truth', $a24A$Correctly identifies the medulla as crest-derived but wrongly extends the crest origin to the mesodermal cortex, predicting loss of the whole gland.$a24A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a24B$Swaps which region depends on neural crest migration, treating the mesodermal cortex as crest-dependent and the medulla as crest-independent.$a24B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a24C$Recognizes the sympathetic ganglia as crest-derived and lost but fails to extend the same crest origin to the medullary chromaffin cells, sparing the medulla.$a24C$ FROM q;

-- Q25 [Neurulation & Neural Crest | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub25$Neurulation & Neural Crest$sub25$, $qt25$A folate-deficient teratogen prevents the neuroectoderm-derived neural folds from closing in the posterior embryo, so the structure that should have enclosed there remains open to the surface. Identifying the affected tissue by its embryonic germ-layer origin, which structure is MOST directly left exposed by this lesion?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "The spinal-cord region of the central nervous system, an ectoderm-derived structure, remains exposed as an open spinal defect (spina bifida)."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The vertebral connective tissue surrounding the cord, a mesoderm-derived structure, remains exposed because closure forms the bony arch first."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The posterior lining of the digestive tract, an endoderm-derived structure, remains exposed because the gut tube and neural tube close together."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The paraxial muscle flanking the cord, a mesoderm-derived structure, remains exposed because somite muscle arises from the closing folds."}]$op25$::jsonb, 'A', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers'. The answer is A because the tissue that fails to enclose is neural tissue, and the nervous system is a derivative of the ectoderm germ layer, so the directly exposed structure is the ectoderm-derived spinal cord. The text states that the ectoderm "develops into the integumentary system (the skin) and the nervous system," and that the posterior neural tube is where "the spinal cord forms," so its failure to close is the textbook origin of spina bifida. Because the lesion strikes the neuroectoderm-derived folds, the structure left open is the CNS itself; structures assigned to the mesoderm (muscle, connective tissue) and the endoderm (digestive lining) are different germ-layer derivatives and are not the tissue that fails to enclose here. (Choice A) is correct: the ectoderm gives rise to the nervous system, so failed closure of the posterior neural tube leaves the spinal-cord region of the CNS exposed, the hallmark of spina bifida. (Choice B) is a partial truth because vertebral connective tissue is genuinely mesoderm-derived and can be involved in spina bifida occulta, but it is not the structure the lesion directly leaves exposed—the open defect is the neural (ectodermal) tissue, not the bony arch. (Choice C) is wrong because the digestive-tract lining is an endoderm derivative formed from a separate tube, so it is neither ectodermal nor the structure exposed by failed neural-fold closure. (Choice D) is wrong because paraxial (somite) muscle is a mesoderm derivative that arises independently of neural-fold closure, so it is not the tissue left open by this ectodermal lesion. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires tracing an affected structure back to its specific germ-layer origin and discriminating an ectoderm derivative from mesoderm- and endoderm-derived alternatives.$ex25$, 'hard', '2C', $cc25$Gastrulation & Germ Layers$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
